the saptune SAP Note or solution definitions, which are present in the
Package Area but differ from the files in the Working Area.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/config/cloud.json\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
the file where saptune stores the detected cloud instance.
.br
Please use \*(Aq\fBsaptune cloud\fP\*(Aq command instead of editing the file
directly.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/etc/sysconfig/saptune\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
the central saptune configuration file in SLES for SAP \fB12 and 15\fP
containing the information about the currently enabled notes and
solutions, the order in which these notes are applied and the version of
saptune currently used.
.br
ATTENTION: the manual editing of this file will be \fBdeprecated\fP on SLES
for SAP 12 and 15 as the main configuration file will be (re)moved in
SLE 16.
.br
Please use the \*(Aq\fBsaptune configure\fP\*(Aq command instead of editing the file
directly.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/var/lib/saptune/config/saptune\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
the central saptune configuration file in SLES and SLES for SAP \fB16\fP
containing the information about the currently enabled notes and
solutions, the order in which these notes are applied and the version of
saptune currently used.
.br
ATTENTION: Do not edit the file directly! Use the \*(Aq\fBsaptune configure\fP\*(Aq
command instead.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
vendor or customer specific tuning or solution definitions.
.br
Please see \fBVENDOR SUPPORT\fP above for more information.
.sp
You can use \*(Aq\fBsaptune note create NoteID\fP\*(Aq or \*(Aq\fBsaptune solution create
solutionName\fP\*(Aq to create a new vendor or customer specific definition or
\*(Aq\fBsaptune note edit NoteID\fP\*(Aq or \*(Aq\fBsaptune solution edit solutionName\fP\*(Aq
to modify an already existing vendor or customer specific definition.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/etc/saptune/override\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
the saptune Note and solution definition override location.
.sp
If you need to customize the Note or solution definitions found in
\fI/usr/share/saptune/notes\fP or \fI/usr/share/saptune/sols\fP or in
\fI/etc/saptune/extra\fP, you can copy them to \fI/etc/saptune/override\fP and
modify them as you need. Please stay with the original name of the Note
or solution definition (the NoteID or solution name) and do \fBNOT\fP rename
it.
.sp
If the shipped Note or Solution does \fBNOT\fP contain an untagged section
an override will not work.
.sp
Or use \*(Aq\fBsaptune note customize NoteID\fP\*(Aq or \*(Aq\fBsaptune solution customize
solutionName\fP\*(Aq to do the job for you.
.br
.RE
.ll
.sp
\fI/run/saptune/saved_state/\fP \fI/run/saptune/parameter/\fP
.RS 3
.ll -.6i
.sp
saptune was designed to preserve the state of the system before starting
the SAP specific tuning, so that it will be possible to restore this
previous state of the system, if the SAP specific tuning is no longer
needed or should be changed.
.sp
This system state is saved during the \*(Aqapply\*(Aq operation of saptune in
the saptune internal used files in /run/saptune/saved_state and
/run/saptune/parameter. The content of these files highly depends on the
previous state of the system.
.br
If the values are applied by saptune, no further monitoring of the
system parameters are done, so changes of saptune relevant parameters
will not be observed. If a SAP Note or a SAP solution should be
reverted, then first the values read from the /run/saptune/saved_state
and /run/saptune/parameter files will be applied to the system to
restore the previous system state and then the corresponding save_state
file will be removed.
.sp
Please do not change or remove files in this directory. The knowledge
about the previous system state gets lost and the revert functionality
of saptune will be destructed. So you will lose the capability to revert
back the tunings saptune has done.
.br
.RE
.ll
.SH "NOTE"
.sp
Using saptune within a pipe, the color information will be removed from
the output.
.br
It is possible to change this behavior by using the command line option
\fB\-\-force\-color\fP
.SH "NOTE"
.sp
When the values from the saptune Note definitions are applied to the
system, no further monitoring of the system parameters are done. So
changes of saptune relevant parameters by using the \*(Aqsysctl\*(Aq command or
by editing configuration files will not be observed. If the values set
by saptune should be reverted, these unrecognized changed settings will
be overwritten by the previous saved system settings from saptune.
.SH "NOTE"
.sp
To prevent unintended reload/restart of saptune during package
installation/update of saptune or normal work, which will result in a
short time period, where the system is not tuned for SAP workloads, it\(cqs
possible to set the parameter \fBIGNORE_RELOAD\fP in the central saptune
configuration file \fI/etc/sysconfig/saptune\fP.
.br
\fBIGNORE_RELOAD\fP is used to control the \*(Aq\fBsystemctl reload
saptune.service\fP\*(Aq behavior.
.br
Default is \fBIGNORE_RELOAD="no"\fP, which means that the \*(Aqreload\*(Aq is
working as expected.
.br
If set to \*(Aq\fByes\fP\*(Aq a \*(Aq\fBsystemctl reload saptune.service\fP\*(Aq and a \*(Aq\fBsaptune
service restart\fP\*(Aq will do \fBnothing\fP. The reason will be logged.
.br
Additional this parameter setting will prevent \*(Aq\fBsystemctl restart
saptune.service\fP\*(Aq (which is a \*(AqExecStop\*(Aq followed by \*(AqExecStart\*(Aq) called
from macros used during the package installation/update of the saptune
package from restarting the tuning.
.br
ATTENTION: Outside the saptune package installation \*(Aq\fBsystemctl restart
saptune.service\fP\*(Aq can \fBnot\fP be blocked.
.SH "ATTENTION"
.sp
Higher or lower system values set by the system, the SAP installer or by
the administrator using sysctl command or sysctl configuration files
will be now \fBoverwritten\fP by saptune, if they are part of the applied
Note definitions.
.sp
saptune now sets the values read from the Note definition files
irrespective of already set higher system values. If you need other
tuning values as defined in the Note definition files, please use the
possibility to create \fBoverride\fP files, which contain the values you
need.
